Triaged **ISSUE-083** — escalated, nothing touched.

**Classification: ambiguous** (DIR-023 vs DIR-026).

The substance: `resolveGitURL` (`add.go:95`) hardcodes `https://github.com/…`, and it's the single choke point every remote flow funnels through. It was inert until today — pre-SPEC-055 the remote path panicked — so shipping the real cloner plus DIR-027's `<org>/<pack-name>` identity convention just made "publish to GitHub or you can't author an installable pack" a live constraint. It also contradicts a premise BUNDLE-006 DD-31 already accepted ("packs may be hosted anywhere") for lock recording.

**My recommendation: DIR-023, not DIR-026.** DIR-026 is a launch blocker with a narrow remainder (REQ-039, transactions/parity) and its charter — make remote consumption *work* — is discharged; appending non-blocking capability expansion dilutes the blocker. DIR-023 is queued and explicitly framed "on the road to the registry era," and ISSUE-083 shares one root cause with its ISSUE-058 thread: core has no data source for where a pack lives, so it bakes one. A registry is the thing that retires the hardcode. Alternative worth your call: a standalone "pack ecosystem openness" directive pairing it with BUNDLE-001 OQ-30 (non-GitHub *publisher* support, still open) — resolving the consume side without the publish side leaves the story half-open.

